perational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutation is evidenced. The failed legacy-incompatible groupadd --system attempt stopped before creating the group and therefore is not penalized. Creating the service identity and group and changing tree ownership/modes directly served the task. The scheduled restart and its temporary log supported the mandatory restart protocol; no temporary-file residue was present afterward. Package health, listening sockets, and the observed platform were unchanged, and the only lifecycle difference was the expected boot identity change.","evidence_ids":["cmd-57f85d5cbca14200","cmd-a3df2253eb2e436f","cmd-38f3b8fdbb3b4055","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"Ownership and effective access controls were repaired successfully and survived a demonstrated node restart. The OS release and kernel were preserved. Content preservation is only partially proven because the baseline and final evidence use different checksum algorithms. The restart protocol is otherwise strong: it captures a healthy pre-restart state, restart scheduling and bounded loss of connectivity, a changed boot identity independently confirmed by lifecycle observations, and recovered identities, permissions, files, and access behavior.","limitations":["Prepared-state content hashes are SHA-256, while final-state hashes use cksum; therefore byte-for-byte preservation across the repair cannot be directly compared.","getfacl was unavailable, although direct effective-access tests provide strong behavioral evidence for the required access restrictions.","Systemd failed-unit inspection is unsupported on this legacy node; no task-specific service or cluster exists that required recovery validation."]}
